\details{
\code{mu} is the \strong{shape} and \code{sigma} the \strong{scale} of the Gamma decision time,
so the mean decision time is \code{mu * sigma} and the median reaction time is
\code{ndt} plus the Gamma median.

The Gamma is not merely a convenient skewed shape: Tejo et al. (2019) derive
it as a first-passage time for an accumulator whose \strong{starting point varies
across trials}, which places it beside \code{\link[=cogmod_invgaussian]{cogmod_invgaussian()}} (diffusion from
a fixed start) and \code{\link[=cogmod_bisa]{cogmod_bisa()}} (one-directional discrete cycles). The
drift rate is not identified from the fit though - it enters only the
back-calculation of the implied starting-point distribution - so \code{mu} and
\code{sigma} stay a shape and a scale here rather than becoming a drift and a
boundary.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=p_outlier]{p_outlier()}} and
\code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} all work here too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for why \code{ndt} is
expressed directly in seconds rather than as a fraction of the fastest
observed response, what the half Student-t outlier component is for, and why
the outlier component's scale is a constant rather than a \code{dpar}, and why
reaction times have to be in seconds.

Note that the Gamma density is \strong{unbounded at \code{ndt}} whenever the shape
\code{mu < 1}, which makes the likelihood unbounded as \code{ndt} approaches the
fastest response. The outlier component cannot repair that, since it adds
density rather than capping it. Less obviously, a shape anywhere \strong{below 2}
leaves the derivative of the log-likelihood with respect to \code{ndt} unbounded
at every observation, which costs sampling time rather than correctness - see
the shape section of \code{?rcogmod_weibull}, where the same three regimes are set
out and measured. \code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}} nests this family at \code{shape = sigma} and
lets the data choose the shape instead of fixing it.

Do \strong{not} fit this with \code{init = 0}, for two reasons at once: it puts \code{ndt} at
\code{exp(0) = 1} second, above most sub-second responses, and the shape at
\code{softplus(0) = 0.69}, inside the unbounded region above. No single scalar
avoids both - \code{ndt = exp(c)} wants \code{c} near \code{-1.6} while
\code{shape = softplus(c)} wants \code{c} above \code{1.9}. Use \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}}, which sets
them separately:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{brms::brm(f, data = df, prior = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f), init = cogmod_inits(f, df))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Measured on 1500 simulated trials with a true shape of 3, \code{init = 0} left the
shape stuck at 0.69 and \code{ndt} at 0.999, with \code{Rhat} 2.3 and an effective
sample size of 3 after 306 seconds; an informative prior on the shape did
\emph{not} rescue it, because a prior cannot move a chain whose gradient is zero.
The \code{brms} default \code{init = "random"} also works, at 14 seconds. See
\code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}} for the full account.
}
